WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.980
The Incredibles 2's opening weekend may have
been a whirlwind success for Disney, but the

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.879
studio was met with a bit of unexpected backlash
once social media users began to voice their

00:00:09.879 --> 00:00:13.179
concerns about one particular element of the
film.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.059
"Looks normal to me…

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:16.059
What the!"

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:19.720
After one Twitter thread brought attention
to some intense flashing light sequences in

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.349
the film, Disney decided to issue a statement
warning viewers that the sequel features moments

00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:28.960
that could potentially trigger those with
light sensitivities, particularly light-sensitive

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.080
epilepsy.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.590
It all started when Twitter user Veronica
Lewis spoke up on Friday night to warn potential

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:38.160
Incredibles 2 audience members about what
goes down in the flick.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.650
She explained that the film includes flashing
strobe lights that are used for extended periods

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.650
throughout the movie.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.790
She also suggested that those who suffer from
issues like migraines, seizure conditions,

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:52.620
vertigo, and other conditions, may experience
a negative reaction to the imagery.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.290
Lewis was quick to point out that the flashing
and strobing light effects are, quote, "an

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:01.150
important point in the plot," since the film's
villain, Screenslaver, uses flashes of light

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.870
to disorient and control his victims.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.390
After Lewis' thread circulated, and others
agreed with her that Disney should have done

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.670
more to warn viewers, the company stepped
up to the plate and issued a formal warning

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.540
to be hung in theaters which reads:

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.830
"Incredibles 2 contains a sequence of flashing
lights which may affect customers who are

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:22.100
susceptible to photosensitive epilepsy or
other photosensitivities."

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.870
While the potential health hazard that could
result from these scenes in Incredibles 2

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:30.490
has not been confirmed, the studio took the
concerns seriously enough to act anyway.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.460
The Epilepsy Foundation also released its
own statement to warn fans of the potential

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.170
for the Disney-Pixar film to induce seizures.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.479
Incredibles 2 isn't even the first animation
to come with a warning.

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:45.640
Who can forget the infamous Pokemon Panic
of 1997, in which an episode of the hit cartoon

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.040
show had to be pulled from the airwaves after
it actually did induce seizures in hundreds

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.299
of Japanese children who watched its flashing
light fight sequence unfold on-screen.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:58.970
Even with this new bit of sudden pushback,
though, Incredibles 2 is still on track to

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:04.450
be a major success for the House of Mouse,
after having a record-breaking $180 million

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.450
opening weekend.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.610
But now, kids and adult audiences alike should
consider themselves warned that the film's

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.870
flashes may be more intense than previously
expected.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:13.870
Thanks for watching!

